  "summary": "Single-nucleus and long-range contact datasets now link cell-type regulatory maps to disease and public reference resources.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread in this set is resolving chromatin organization and enhancer-TF regulatory programs at cell-type resolution. A contact atlas stratifies long-range chromatin contacts across human tissues by cell-type persistence and links persistent contacts to AT-rich sequence determinants of 3D genome organization. Separately, single-nucleus multi-omics compares enhancer and TF-binding programs in Pick's and Alzheimer's disease, identifying shared and distinct pathways, and releases the scROAD database. What is new is the joining of cell-type-resolved regulatory maps to disease contexts and to public reference resources. The remaining preprints address genetic intervention sets, protein-barcoded imaging CRISPR screens, and a statistical test for genetic convergence in Perturb-seq; these are methodological and share this scope only partly.",
